1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:07,522
Embedding Pulse: the engine inside your own program.

2
00:00:08,322 --> 00:00:16,031
Most films so far have used the C L I or the M C P server. Both are thin faces over one Go library.

3
00:00:16,831 --> 00:00:24,071
In your own program, pulse dot New builds an instance, and Process runs a request on it, as many times as you like.

4
00:00:24,871 --> 00:00:31,066
Its options set the data directory, limits and defaults, your extensions, and the observability hooks.

5
00:00:31,866 --> 00:00:37,314
New checks all of it once, at startup. If anything is wrong, you get an error and no instance.

6
00:00:38,114 --> 00:00:47,509
Options dot Extensions takes your own operators, of most kinds today: from aggregators and filters to tests, matrix operators and regressions.

7
00:00:48,309 --> 00:00:56,616
It also takes expression functions, tables, populations, and skills and examples for agents. More are added over time.

8
00:00:57,416 --> 00:01:01,819
Every name follows one pattern: category, your namespace, then the name.

9
00:01:02,619 --> 00:01:07,790
The stock binary registers nothing, so its manifest lists empty extension slots.

10
00:01:08,590 --> 00:01:15,937
Here's a real one: a midrange aggregator in the ACME namespace, halfway between the smallest and largest value.

11
00:01:16,737 --> 00:01:23,636
It declares that it streams and merges, so it implements the online and mergeable interfaces from package extend.

12
00:01:24,436 --> 00:01:28,263
The manifest now lists it, with its namespace and what it accepts.

13
00:01:29,063 --> 00:01:36,730
And a request uses it like a built-in. By region, it sits beside min and max: East is sixty-three point two nine.

14
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:41,783
It declared no component schema, so its components carry the floor alone.

15
00:01:42,583 --> 00:01:50,570
Expression functions and lookup tables extend formulas. Here, a with tax function, and a tax rate table by region.

16
00:01:51,370 --> 00:01:56,711
A formula calls both, row by row. Filter expressions see the same environment.

17
00:01:57,511 --> 00:02:01,444
By region, each gross sum is the net plus that region's tax.

18
00:02:02,244 --> 00:02:06,049
The manifest lists the function, with its signature, and the table.

19
00:02:06,849 --> 00:02:13,300
Before New returns, it builds each factory from a bare spec over an empty schema, and checks what comes back.

20
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:19,932
A factory that claims to stream but returns no streaming interface is a streamable mismatch.

21
00:02:20,732 --> 00:02:25,689
A factory that assumes a schema panics, and the probe reports that panic as an error.

22
00:02:26,489 --> 00:02:30,678
A built-in's name collides, and a name outside the pattern is invalid.

23
00:02:31,478 --> 00:02:37,054
Either way, there is no instance. A broken extension fails at startup, not mid-request.

24
00:02:37,854 --> 00:02:45,499
Some bugs only show up on real rows. This aggregator writes to a nil map whenever an amount is over seventy-five.

25
00:02:46,299 --> 00:02:53,155
Process doesn't crash. It returns a processing panic error, with the operation, the panic's type and the stack.

26
00:02:53,955 --> 00:02:58,016
The panic value itself is dropped, because it could carry row data.

27
00:02:58,816 --> 00:03:03,005
The same instance answers the next request, four rows, as before.

28
00:03:03,805 --> 00:03:07,653
If your host would rather crash, set disable panic recovery.

29
00:03:08,453 --> 00:03:15,074
Process stream result hands you the result as it is produced: a header, a channel of chunks, and a terminator.

30
00:03:15,874 --> 00:03:21,087
The header comes before any row, with the request's hash and a best-effort size estimate.

31
00:03:21,887 --> 00:03:28,423
This grouped request sends four chunks, one per region. Only the last carries the per-group components.

32
00:03:29,223 --> 00:03:35,034
A group exists only once every record is read, so grouped rows arrive at the final flush.

33
00:03:35,834 --> 00:03:41,837
The terminator says completed, four rows, and the terminal components equal a buffered Process run.

34
00:03:42,637 --> 00:03:49,941
Watch reports changes to a cohort file on a channel. WatchDir does the same for every cohort under a folder.

35
00:03:50,741 --> 00:03:56,530
It polls: a stat, then a content hash, every two hundred and fifty milliseconds by default.

36
00:03:57,330 --> 00:04:01,114
On the first tick, a file that's already there shows up as created.

37
00:04:01,914 --> 00:04:06,125
Copy a bigger cohort over it, and you get modified, with a new hash.

38
00:04:06,925 --> 00:04:12,906
Delete it, and you get removed. A host can re-run its requests whenever a file is modified.

39
00:04:13,706 --> 00:04:19,965
Request dot hash gives a cache key. The same request with its keys in another order hashes the same.

40
00:04:20,765 --> 00:04:23,781
Group by channel instead, and the hash changes.

41
00:04:24,581 --> 00:04:30,285
It's canonical JSON, so it's stable across processes, and the return block is part of it.

42
00:04:31,085 --> 00:04:38,666
Filter to file with request names its output by the source's hash and the predicate's, so the same filter again is reused.

43
00:04:39,466 --> 00:04:44,871
A predicate that calls your own function is never reused. It re-runs on every call.

44
00:04:45,671 --> 00:04:53,551
Expression reads parses one expression and lists the columns, params and functions it names. No instance, no cohort.

45
00:04:54,351 --> 00:04:58,647
It scopes the way the engine does, so a let-bound name is not a column.

46
00:04:59,447 --> 00:05:03,956
An expression that doesn't parse is a compile error, with an empty read set.

47
00:05:04,756 --> 00:05:13,148
Request reads does it for a whole request: every slot that reads data, with its path, its key, and the columns and params it reads.

48
00:05:13,948 --> 00:05:21,999
Options dot hooks fire around every operation. The start hook gets the kind, the scope, the cohort and the request hash.

49
00:05:22,799 --> 00:05:26,775
The phase hook reports each phase that ran, in order, with its time.

50
00:05:27,575 --> 00:05:33,791
The end hook gets the code, the arm the engine chose, and the row counts: twenty-four scanned, four out.

51
00:05:34,591 --> 00:05:40,274
A refused request ends after planning and opening, with its error code, never its message.

52
00:05:41,074 --> 00:05:44,922
Hooks must be fast. A panic inside one is recovered too.

53
00:05:45,722 --> 00:05:52,599
Pulse ships adapters for both. Observe prom turns its metrics into Prometheus counters and histograms.

54
00:05:53,399 --> 00:06:01,044
Observe otel turns its hooks into Open Telemetry spans, with one event per phase, and an error status on a refusal.

55
00:06:01,844 --> 00:06:09,980
You don't need Go for either: Pulse M C P serves Prometheus metrics, and exports O T L P to the standard endpoint.

56
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:16,335
After one tool call, the scrape counts it, and the collector receives its traces and metrics.

57
00:06:17,135 --> 00:06:23,948
That's embedding Pulse today: build an instance, extend it, guard it, react to it, plan against it, and watch it run.

58
00:06:24,748 --> 00:06:31,412
More is added over time, and the manifest lists what your instance offers, your own extensions included.

59
00:06:32,212 --> 00:06:39,175
And that's the end of Pulse Anatomy: twenty-three films, from one request to your own program. Thanks for watching.

60
00:06:39,975 --> 00:06:41,242
Pulse Anatomy.
